  "question": {
    "text": "A variable line passes through the fixed point $(\\alpha, \\beta) .$ The locus of the foot of the perpendicular from the origin on the line is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "eBdIj",
      "text": "$x^{2}+y^{2}-\\alpha x-\\beta y=0$",
      "isCorrect": true,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "gm1bm",
      "text": "$x^{2}-y^{2}+2 a x+2 \\beta y=0$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "wiU8q",
      "text": "$\\alpha x+\\beta y \\pm \\sqrt{\\left(\\alpha^{2}+\\beta^{2}\\right)}=0$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    },
    {
      "id": "t3I8H",
      "text": "$\\frac{x^{2}}{\\alpha^{2}}+\\frac{y^{2}}{\\beta^{2}}=1$",
      "isCorrect": false,
      "image": null
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let $(\\alpha, \\beta)$ be the given point, let $Q(x, y)$ be the foot of the perpendicular, and let $O$ be the origin. The line can have any direction\r\n$$\r\n\\angle P Q O=90^{\\circ}\r\n$$\r\nPoint $Q$ lies on the circle having diameter $O P$.\r\nThe locus of point $Q$.\r\n$$\r\n(x-0)(x-\\alpha)+(y-0)(y-\\beta)=0\r\n$$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad x^{2}-x \\alpha+y^{2}-y \\beta=0$\r\n$$\r\n\\Rightarrow \\quad x^{2}+y^{2}-\\alpha x-\\beta y=0\r\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
